From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Smith <scott@newtrostudios.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:30:14 -0400
Subject: remotesmith(0801): verified file-transfer finalization and
 failed-item reporting

AC-18, AC-48, DEF-6 (whole-product review finding 2). Upstream dropped
write errors on the controller (io_loop "to-do"), renamed the
.download staging file to the final name without checking it and
ignored rename failures, so a full disk could leave a partial file under
the final name with a "done" status.

- TransferJob::write records the first open/write/finalize failure,
  removes that file's staging data and refuses every later block, so a
  gap is never covered by the blocks after it.
- Finalizing a file (on switch to the next file and when the sender is
  done) flushes and syncs it, which surfaces deferred write errors,
  requires the sender's end-of-file block (every file ends with an empty
  block), that the staging file holds every byte written and exactly the
  size the sender listed, and only then renames it into place. The
  protocol carries no content hash. A source that changed size after it
  was listed fails that file ("source changed during transfer or data
  lost"). A compressed block that does not decompress fails the job
  (upstream's decompress returned an empty buffer). Any failure removes
  the staging file this job created; verified data whose rename failed
  (e.g. a locked destination) stays staged through later failures and
  cancellation, and staging the job did not create is never removed.
- Controller downloads: a write failure cancels the job at the sender and
  reports it failed with the file name and OS error; a Done that crosses
  the cancel is ignored, so a failed item never flips to done. Done runs
  the verified finalize before reporting.
- Host uploads (CM): NewWrite now carries each file's size; a write
  failure fails the job once and drops it; WriteDone reports an error
  instead of done when finalizing fails.
- When the sender reports an error, a fully received last file is kept
  (verified) and a partial one removed.
- Stale staging: when the receiver decides to skip a file, a
  .download/.digest pair whose digest matches that file's listed size and
  mtime (an interrupted transfer of the same source) is removed; nothing
  is removed before the decision. Writing a file replaces an existing
  pair only if its .digest is RustDesk's; other files at those names fail
  the item ("not overwritten") instead of being clobbered.
- Failures are terminal: once a job is reported failed (own write or
  finalize failure, or an Error from the sender), a later Done or Error
  for it is ignored, so e.g. a host that sends Error then Done for an
  unreadable last file never shows success. This applies to transfer
  jobs only; file operations (remove, create, rename) reuse their id for
  later replies and are not affected.

Tests through the real handlers: client::io_loop::tests (complete
download, sender Error then Done, short data with end-of-file, rename
failure while advancing plus cancel, corrupt compressed block, skip
decision with user and stale staging files, a failed file operation
followed by a success under the same id) and
ui_cm_interface::tests::upload_is_verified_before_done. Unit tests
(libs/base, fs::tests): complete files renamed with mtime, short
file never finalized, short earlier file fails on switch and later blocks
refused, unwritable destination, rename failure, file that changed size,
resume completion and changed-source refusal, stale staging cleanup,
and an ignored macOS test that fills a 5 MB hdiutil disk image.

